    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat from inside to outside, using a special fluid called refrigerant. A compressor squeezes the refrigerant, making it hot. That hot fluid flows through coils on the back or bottom, where it releases heat into your kitchen and cools down. Then it passes through a tiny valve that drops the pressure, making it suddenly very cold. Those cold coils inside the fridge absorb heat from your food and air. The refrigerant carries that heat back to the compressor, and the cycle repeats. Insulation and a sealed door help trap the cold inside, so your food stays chilled."
